WEBVTT

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:21.800
So in suite number 15, based on everything we just learned previously, we said that when it comes to going to a restaurant where an irreligious Jewish waiter will be the one to open the bottle of wine and possibly even pouring the wine or shaking the wine, so we said the best thing is to avoid it and don't rely on all the leniencies that we said before because it's avoidable.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:26.580
however if it's unavoidable and the waiter will be offended to tell him that he's not allowed to

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:31.820
open your bottle so then we said there are there is grounds to allow it if a irreligious parent

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:36.560
would recite Kiddush drink from the cup and then offer the wine to their religious child again the

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:40.740
best thing would be is to avoid drinking it if it's impossible there are there is ground to

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:48.480
be lenient to drink it as well okay now when we discuss the concept of Nitzuk so Nitzuk is the

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:55.920
The definition of Nitzok is when kosher wine is poured into a cup or a container which contains non-kosher wine

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:04.140
and the stream that is going between the bottle which is pouring into the container or the cup is completely unbroken.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.420
It is an unbroken stream.

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:15.840
In this scenario, we view it as if the wine which is inside the cup or the container is now inside of the bottle which was being poured from into

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:22.860
and potentially can forbid the bottle itself that is being poured out of now the question is what kind

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:28.620
of what type of wine are we talking about that's inside of the cup so first of all the the wine

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:34.660
whatever it is it must be wet enough that it would be uh it's wide enough to get your hand wet which

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:39.440
could then wet something else and also when we say that it's non-kosher wine so the Shach told us that

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:44.140
has to be that it's wine which is forbidden both from drinking and from benefit if it's only

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:05.560
The Rama does say that Nitzuk would apply to wine which is touched by a Christian.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:11.120
If the wine in the cup would only be prohibited from being in contact with a Muslim or an irreligious Jew though,

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:15.220
so then Nitzuk does not apply because it is certainly permitted to benefit from that wine

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:17.740
and therefore Nitzuk does not apply to that scenario.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:23.140
In a case of great loss of money where the bottle that was being poured from was very expensive,

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:29.920
so then the Shulchan Aruch said that we would allow the bottle that was poured from, we would not apply Nitzuk.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:34.240
The Rama did qualify those by saying there has to be a case of significant loss.

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:39.920
If it's just a small amount of wine or it's inexpensive wine, so then we would apply Nitzuk and we would forbid the bottle that was poured from.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:47.200
We also mentioned another leniency from the Rama, that the Rama said we can use the concept of bitul peshishim in the context of Nitzuk.

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:56.000
So again, so we view as if the contents of the cup that are being poured into are now inside the bottle that is being poured from.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:24.700
bitul b'shishim

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.300
We're relying on the fact that the bottle will be 60 times the wine that's inside the cup.

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:32.080
That would be Mevat Ezzel Chachila, which we say is prohibited.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:37.800
If let's say a person was pouring kosher Mevushal wine into a cup which is containing forbidden wine,

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:40.180
it still could become prohibited.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:47.340
The Mevushal wine can become prohibited because although it's Mevushal and protected from being touched by a non-Jew,

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:53.260
again, because Nitzuk is viewed as if the wine in the cup is being mixed with the wine in the bottle,

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:56.780
then being Mavushel would not protect it

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:00.280
if a non-Jew would ever pour wine to dispose of it

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:01.140
they're pouring it down the drain

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:02.600
so then the wine would be permitted

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:06.020
that's not subject to the rules of Stam Yeinam

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:11.480
also if a non-Jew would touch wine with the sole intent of making it forbidden to Jews

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:14.820
they were really trying to make the Jews upset and ask for their wine

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:15.960
so then it would be permitted

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:17.920
and it should even be drunk in front of them

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:19.840
to show them that they were not successful

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:27.280
We then introduced the topic of leaving wine unattended with non-Jews.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:32.820
So there are two real concerns when leaving wine unsupervised with a non-Jew.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:38.800
First of all, we are concerned that the non-Jew may touch the wine with the intent to libate and therefore can prohibit the wine that way.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:47.220
Also, we are concerned that perhaps the non-Jew exchanged the kosher wine with forbidden wine, and now the wine inside of the bottle is actually prohibited.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:05:10.720
Regarding this second concern that the non-Jew had exchanged the wine, so there has to be, this does take some time for the non-Jew to go get forbidden wine and then to make that exchange, and therefore we assume it would take a little bit longer than a mil for him to do that, in which case, again, we would not allow wine to be left with a non-Jew, especially not for a time that's longer than a mil.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:18.180
However, if let's say a person did leave an open bottle of wine with a non-Jew, unintended.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:24.740
So in a case where the non-Jew was a Christian or a bona fide idol worshiper,

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:30.400
so then the Shulchan Aruch says that the wine is forbidden, even if you just left for a few seconds.

00:05:30.520 --> 00:05:34.440
Again, the concern is that the Christian or idol worshiper had touched the bottle of wine

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:38.280
and made it forbidden because he had intent to libate.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:48.820
The Rama did say that because Christians nowadays have the status of Muslims and are non-idol worshippers, he does not go as far as Shulchan Aruch, and he would not prohibit the wine immediately, it would take much longer.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:06:07.880
But in a situation where the wine was left with a Christian, according to the Rama, or even according to Shulchan Aruch, if it was left with a Muslim, or an irreligious Jew, or any other non-idol worshipping individual, so if it's in the home of a Jew, and there's no other non-kosher wine around, so the concern is that maybe they switched it with non-kosher wine.

00:06:07.880 --> 00:06:22.620
In which case, it would only be prohibited if it was left with them for the time that it takes to walk a mill, which is more than 18 minutes or more than 24 minutes, but anything less than that, we would not be concerned that they had swapped out the wine.

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:29.800
If there is a concern that the non-Jew may drink from the wine or make direct contact with it for their own benefit,

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:39.180
so then again the wine becomes forbidden immediately, assuming that they are someone who would cause the wine to be prohibited if they would come in direct contact with it.
